.TH CINQ 5 2026-10-31 cinq "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
cinq \- configuration file for the keys of cinq
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cinq/config.toml
.br
.I $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/cinq/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cinq
is a cue-based show controller in the spirit of QLab, and the keyboard
runs most of it.
It ships two keymaps, each of which binds keys to every action, and a
configuration file in TOML chooses one of them, or builds keymaps of its
own, and binds actions to other keys.
.B cinq
reads the file when it starts and again whenever it changes, so that
the keys, and the help page that lists them, follow each change as soon
as it is saved.
.PP
The help page, which the key of
.B help
opens, names the keymap in use, lists every action
with its keys and its name in the file, and tells which file the keys
came from.
.SH FILES
.B cinq
reads the first of these files that exists, and no other:
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cinq/config.toml
The file in the directory of the user's configuration, which is
.I ~/.config
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or is not an absolute path.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/cinq/config.toml
The file in each directory of the system's configuration in turn.
.B XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
lists these directories separated by colons, of which relative paths
count for nothing, and the list is
.I /etc/xdg
when it is unset or holds no absolute path.
.PP
These directories follow the XDG Base Directory Specification on every
POSIX system.
On Windows, the file is
.I cinq\e\:config.toml
in the folder of roaming application data,
.IR %APPDATA% ,
such as
.IR C:\eUsers\eAlice\eAppData\eRoaming .
.PP
Making or deleting any of these files applies while
.B cinq
runs as well, so that deleting the file in use brings back the next one
that exists, or else the keys of vi.
.PP
Started with
.BI \-\-config\-dir= directory\fR,
.B cinq
reads
.I directory/config.toml
in place of all of these files, and follows it as it changes in the
same way.
.SH SETTINGS
.TP
.B keymap
Name of the keymap that the keys start from: one that
.B cinq
ships,
.B vi
or
.BR cua ,
as
.B KEYMAPS
describes them, or one of the file's own under
.BR keymaps .
It is
.B vi
unless the file names another.
.TP
.B keymaps
Table of keymaps of the file's own, each of them a table named after its
keymap.
The
.B from
of such a keymap names the keymap that it starts from, a shipped one or
another of the file's own, and without
.B from
it starts with no keys at all.
Its other entries bind actions to keys as those of
.B keys
do.
A keymap of the file's own cannot take the name of a shipped one.
.TP
.B keys
Table that binds actions to keys in the keymap in use.
Each entry is named after an action, as listed under
.BR ACTIONS ,
and holds a key sequence in a string, or several in an array of strings,
any of which carries out the action.
An action that the table names loses the keys that the keymap gives it,
so that an empty array leaves it with no keys at all, and the actions
that the table leaves out keep the keys of the keymap.
.SH KEYMAPS
.TP
.B vi
Keys in the manner of vi: letters move through cues and lists and act on
them, some commands take two keys, such as
.BR "d d" ,
and the arrows and Control with
.B n
and
.B p
move as well.
.TP
.B cua
Keys of IBM's Common User Access, which most desktop programs follow:
the arrows, Home, and End move, Control with a letter edits, and F1
shows the help page, which Control+w closes.
.PP
Both keep GO on the space bar and Panic on Escape, as in QLab.
The table lists the keys that each keymap binds to every action, in the
order of the help page.
.PP
.TS
lb lb lb
l l l.
Action	vi	cua
_
go	Space	Space
panic	Escape	Escape
next	j, ArrowDown, Control+n	ArrowDown
previous	k, ArrowUp, Control+p	ArrowUp
next_list	l, ArrowRight	Control+PageDown
previous_list	h, ArrowLeft	Control+PageUp
first	g g	Home
last	G	End
next_tab	g t	Control+Tab
previous_tab	g T	Control+Shift+Tab
standby	Enter	Enter
edit	i	F2
next_field	Tab	Tab
previous_field	Shift+Tab	Shift+Tab
add_below	o	Control+n
add_above	O	Control+N
delete	d d	Delete
undo	u	Control+z
redo	Control+r	Control+y, Control+Z
help	?	F1
close_help	q	Control+w
.TE
.SH KEYS
A key goes by its value in the W3C's UI Events KeyboardEvent key Values.
.IP \(bu 2
A key that types a character is that character, as in
.BR j ,
.BR G ,
or
.BR ? .
Letters tell the case apart, so that
.B G
is the key that Shift and
.B g
type together.
.IP \(bu 2
Any other key goes by its name, as in
.BR Enter ,
.BR Escape ,
.BR Tab ,
.BR ArrowDown ,
.BR PageUp ,
or
.BR F5 .
.IP \(bu 2
The space bar is
.BR Space ,
since its value, the space it types, would read as the gap between two
keys.
.PP
Control, Alt, and Shift can be held with a key, each joined to it by a
.BR + ,
as in
.BR Control+d ,
.BR Alt+ArrowUp ,
or
.BR Control+Shift+Tab .
A
.B +
with nothing after it is the plus key itself, as in
.BR Control++ .
Shift with a letter is the capital that it types, so that
.B Shift+g
is
.B G
and
.B Control+Shift+n
is
.BR Control+N .
Any other key that types a character is written as the character it
types with Shift held, as in
.B ?
rather than
.BR Shift+/ ,
and Shift cannot be added to it.
Shift can be held with the space bar and with any key that goes by its
name, as in
.B Shift+Space
or
.BR Shift+Tab .
.PP
A modifier key, such as Shift or Control, cannot be bound by itself.
Keys held with the logo key belong to the system, and
.B cinq
leaves them alone, so a key cannot hold Meta or Super.
.PP
A key sequence is one or more keys separated by spaces, which are typed
one after another, as in
.BR "g g" .
.SH TYPING
The keys of a sequence wait until the sequence is complete, and the
status bar shows the keys typed so far, while a box above it lists the
keys that can come next and what each of them does.
A key that begins no sequence is dropped, but a key that breaks off a
sequence typed halfway starts a new one, so that a stray key never
swallows the next.
Anything done other than typing, such as a click, drops a sequence
typed halfway, and so does a change to the file.
.PP
Digits typed before a sequence count it, as in
.B "3 j"
with the vi keymap.
The count repeats
.BR next ,
.BR previous ,
.BR next_list ,
and
.BR previous_list ,
deletes as many cues for
.BR delete ,
and gives the row, counted from one, that
.B first
and
.B last
select.
Other actions leave the count unused.
Since digits count, no sequence can start with one.
.PP
A key bound by itself to
.B go
or
.B panic
acts at once, even halfway through another sequence, and whatever
modifiers are held with it, unless the other of the two is bound to the
key with those very modifiers.
Holding either key down acts only once, so that holding the key of
Panic does not cut a panic short, while every other key repeats as it
is held.
.SH WHERE KEYS ACT
Keys act on the cue list, apart from the keys typed while one of these
has the keyboard:
.TP
A text field of the inspector
The field keeps the keys typed into it, apart from the keys bound by
themselves to
.BR go ,
.BR panic ,
.BR next_field ,
and
.BR previous_field .
Enter leaves the field.
.TP
The help page
Only the keys of
.BR go ,
.BR panic ,
.BR help ,
and
.B close_help
act, and
.B go
and
.B panic
close the page as well, so that the show is in view.
.TP
The menu of a drop-down list
The keys bound by themselves to
.B next
and
.B previous
move the highlight one option at a time, whatever the count, and the
highlight stays put past either end.
Enter picks the highlighted option.
The keys of
.B go
and
.B panic
act as they do everywhere and close the menu as well.
The menu keeps every other key until it closes.
.PP
Show mode locks editing:
.BR edit ,
.BR add_below ,
.BR add_above ,
and
.B delete
do nothing in it, and
.B undo
and
.B redo
only move standby back and forth.
.SH ACTIONS
The actions go by their names in the
.B keys
table, in the order of the help page.
.TP
.B go
GO: fire the standby cue, together with every cue that auto-continue or
auto-follow chains to it.
.TP
.B panic
Panic: fade every playing cue out over one second, and stop the rest.
A second panic while the first is still fading stops everything at once.
.TP
.B next
Select the next cue, or move the highlight of an open menu down.
.TP
.B previous
Select the previous cue, or move the highlight of an open menu up.
.TP
.B next_list
Show the next cue list.
.TP
.B previous_list
Show the previous cue list.
.TP
.B first
Select the first cue, or the cue on the row counted.
.TP
.B last
Select the last cue, or the cue on the row counted.
.TP
.B next_tab
Show the next tab of the inspector.
.TP
.B previous_tab
Show the previous tab of the inspector.
.TP
.B standby
Put the selected cue on standby, so that the next GO fires it.
.TP
.B edit
Edit the name of the selected cue.
.TP
.B next_field
Move the keyboard to the next field of the inspector.
.TP
.B previous_field
Move the keyboard to the previous field of the inspector.
.TP
.B add_below
Add a cue of the kind chosen in the toolbar below the selection, or at
the end of the list without one.
.TP
.B add_above
Add a cue of the kind chosen in the toolbar above the selection, or at
the end of the list without one.
.TP
.B delete
Delete the selected cue, or as many cues from it down as the count.
.TP
.B undo
Undo the latest change to the show.
.TP
.B redo
Redo the change undone last.
.TP
.B help
Show the help page, or hide it.
.TP
.B close_help
Hide the help page.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Problems with the file show among the warnings in the sidebar, each
after
.BR config.toml: ,
and the status bar counts them.
.IP \(bu 2
A file that cannot be read, or that is not TOML, leaves the keys of the
vi keymap.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B keymap
that names no keymap leaves the keys of vi, and a
.B keys
that is not a table leaves those of the keymap.
A keymap of the file's own whose
.B from
names no keymap, or that comes back around to itself through
.BR from ,
starts from no keys, and one named after a shipped keymap goes unused.
.IP \(bu 2
A setting other than
.B keymap
and
.BR keys ,
and an entry of the table that names no action, are ignored, and the
rest of the file applies.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty string, a key that names no key, a sequence that starts with a
digit, and a value that is not a string are left out, and the action
keeps the other keys listed for it, which may be none.
.IP \(bu 2
A sequence bound to two actions goes to the one listed first under
.BR ACTIONS ,
and the other goes without it.
A sequence that begins a longer one, as
.B g
begins
.BR "g g" ,
leaves the longer one impossible to type.
A key bound by itself to
.B go
or
.B panic
makes every other sequence that holds it impossible to type, whatever
modifiers are held with it there, apart from a key that the other of the
two has by itself, as
.B TYPING
describes.
.PP
The help page shows the keys as they ended up.
.SH EXAMPLES
Use the cua keymap, with GO on F5 as well as on the space bar:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
keymap = "cua"

[keys]
go = ["Space", "F5"]
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep the vi keymap, but move through cues and menus with the arrows
alone, and let no key delete a cue:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
[keys]
next = "ArrowDown"
previous = "ArrowUp"
delete = []
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a keymap of one's own from no keys at all, in which only GO,
Panic, and the arrows act:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
keymap = "booth"

[keymaps.booth]
go = "Space"
panic = "Escape"
next = "ArrowDown"
previous = "ArrowUp"
.EE
.RE
